\par\par \b \uc2 Thai Exporter Assocs Call For Ctrl Bk To Curb Baht Rise  \b0\par\par\uc2 348 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2006\par 07:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--A group of 10 Thai exporter associations Thursday issued a joint statement saying they will ask the Bank of Thailand and other related authorities act to ensure the baht doesn't appreciate at a greater pace than regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "We demand that they implement appropriate measures to maintain baht stability in order to maintain the country's competitiveness... The persistent rise in the baht makes us unable to compete in terms of price," the statement said.  \par \par \uc2 Representatives of the 10 associations, which group producers of frozen foods, chicken, shrimp, fruit and vegetables, vegetable oil, garments and other sectors, said they would send a formal statement to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The associations' members account for around THB680 billion ($18.6 billion) of the country's exports each year, according to the joint statement. Thailand's exports have recently hit record highs above $11 billion per month.  \par \par \uc2 The baht has appreciated by around 12% against the U.S. dollar so far this year, while most regional units have risen by 3%-6%, and Chinese yuan has gained only 1%-2%, the statement said.  \par \par \uc2 If the baht continues to strengthen faster than its regional peers, the loss of competitiveness may cause members of the associations to begin reducing production, laying off staff and eventually closing businesses, it said.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets only to reduce daily volatility and speculative activity, not to counter the effects of real trade and investment flows.  \par \par \uc2 Governor Tarisa Watanagase said Wednesday the baht's recent strength has been the result of flows into regional and local stock and bond markets.  \par \par \uc2 She said the baht has outperformed regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar this year after underperforming last year.  \par \par \uc2 At 0556 GMT, the dollar was quoted at THB36.505, off a low Tuesday of THB36.38, it's lowest level since February 1999.  \par \par \uc2 -By Bangkok Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; 662-266-0744; djnews.bangkok@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Paul Baylis [ 16-11-06 0626GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020061116e2bg000qv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
