\par\par \b \uc2 = UPDATE:Thai Exporter Assocs Call For Action Vs Baht Rise  \b0\par\par\uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2006\par 11:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2006, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK, Nov 16, 2006 (DJCS via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 (Updates with supporting comments from finance ministry official, suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday)  \par \par \uc2 A group of 10 Thai exporter associations Thursday issued a joint statement saying they will ask the Bank of Thailand and other related authorities act to ensure the baht doesn't appreciate at a greater pace than regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "We demand that they implement appropriate measures to maintain baht stability in order to maintain the country's competitiveness... The persistent rise in the baht makes us unable to compete in terms of price," the statement said. Chakkrit Parapuntakul, a spokesman for the Public Debt Management Office in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , later sought to reassure exporters by saying that as the threat of inflation has eased and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have room to start cutting interest rates, which could lower demand for the baht.  \par \par \uc2 However, the Bank of Thailand's monetary policy committee had said at its last meeting on Oct. 18 that it remains cautious despite declining inflation. The committee left its 14-day repurchase market target rate unchanged at 5% due to the possibility of renewed volatility in global oil prices and other uncertainties.Comments Wednesday by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Tarisa Watanagase didn't give exporters much encouragement about the possibility of help from official quarters. She said the baht's recent strength has been the result of flows into regional as well as local stock and bond markets, and that the baht has outperformed regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar this year only after underperforming last year.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to only reduce daily volatility and speculative activity, not to counter the effects of real trade and investment flows.  \par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said agents of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  were suspected of buying the dollar Thursday to help prevent a fall toward Tuesday's eight-year low of THB36.38. The dollar closed in Asian trade Thursday at THB36.50, versus THB36.56 Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Chakkrit told reporters that there is no need for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain domestic interest rates at current levels because such rates would continue to attract capital inflows, resulting in a firmer baht.  \par \par \uc2 "Easier interest rates should help reduce appreciation pressure on the baht," said Chakkrit.  \par \par \uc2 The comments may not represent an official ministry position on the subject, however. Finance Minister Pridiyathorn Devakula declined to comment on the matter this week, saying he didn't appreciate interference from the ministry when he was governor, so he wasn't going to interfere himself.  \par \par \uc2 Economists said lower domestic rates probably wouldn't help to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because such a move would bolster sentiment toward the local stock and bond markets, attracting even larger inflows.  \par \par \uc2 The baht has appreciated by around 12% against the U.S. dollar so far this year, while most regional units have risen by 3%-6%, and Chinese yuan has gained only 1%-2%, the exporters' associations said in the statement.  \par \par \uc2 If the baht continues to strengthen faster than its regional peers, the loss of competitiveness may cause members of the associations to begin reducing production, laying off staff and eventually closing businesses, it said.  \par \par \uc2 Representatives of the 10 associations, which group producers of frozen foods, chicken, shrimp, fruit and vegetables, vegetable oil, garments and other sectors, said they would soon be sending a formal statement to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The associations' members account for around THB680 billion ($18.6 billion) of the country's exports each year, according to the joint statement. Thailand's exports have recently hit record highs above $11 billion per month.  \par \par \uc2 -By Phisanu Phromchanya, Dow Jones Newswires; 66 2266 0744; phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires  \par \par \uc2 11-16-06 0547ET  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020061116e2bg000yk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
