\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Thai c.bank says  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  on baht again  \b0\par\par\uc2 277 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 2006\par 06:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK, Oct 30 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the baht again although the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has started to stabilize after inflows slowed, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Tarisa Watanagase said on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "We have still come in to take care of the baht closely. But the situation is better now after inflows have slowed," she told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 The baht <THB=> was quoted at around 36.72 per dollar at 0650 GMT, its strongest since January 2000.  \par \par \uc2 Tarisa said exporters must cope with the stronger baht although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to enter the market.  \par \par \uc2 "We believe exporters can adjust themselves, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will still come in as necessary to prevent the baht from being too volatile," she said.  \par \par \uc2 However, Tarisa said it was not the time to cut interest rates due to uncertainties about inflation and world oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 "It's not the time to cut rates as we are still not sure about inflation," she said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept its 14-day repurchase rate unchanged at 5.0 percent for the third month in a row in October after inflation eased to its lowest in 19 months.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not say how long it would leave rates unchanged, but senior officials told have said oil prices and inflationary pressures would be taken into account before deciding any rate cut. Its next rate meeting is in December.  \par \par \uc2 It raised rates 13 times between August 2004 and June 2006 as policy makers worldwide tightened credit to combat inflation.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-THAILAND-BOT (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061030e2au000d1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
