\par\par \b \uc2 Thai Baht Stabilizes After Coup; Bonds Recover  \b0\par\par\uc2 788 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2006\par 12:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The Thai baht stabilized in Asian trade Thursday, ending well off the lows it fell to against the U.S. dollar after a sudden military coup late Tuesday, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The baht's recovery was driven by exporters' dollar sales and portfolio inflows on optimism that the bloodless coup would bring an efficient resolution to the country's political stalemate.  \par \par \uc2 Long-term government bond prices also plummeted for a brief period after the post-coup opening but ended higher as demand from local funds returned.  \par \par \uc2 Local and foreign funds regained confidence after seeing other markets recover quickly, bond dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at an intraday low of THB37.455, up from Tuesday's close of THB37.27 but recovering from a post-coup high of THB37.95.  \par \par \uc2 Financial markets were closed Wednesday on orders of the Administrative Reform Council, which led the coup to oust Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra. The closure helped to limit panic selling in the early stages of the coup.  \par \par \uc2 "The baht is in the stabilizing stage. From the limited impact in the stock market, it's clear that investors are not too panicked by the coup and not in a hurry to cash out of the country," said a dealer at a local bank.  \par \par \uc2 Many foreign investors were also believed to have participated in the bargain hunting that followed initial losses among Thai shares with solid fundamentals, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Investors shrugged off comments by Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hadn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, as most parties hadn't suspected that the authorities were active anyway.  \par \par \uc2 Army Commander-in-Chief and coup leader Gen. Sondhi Boonyaratkalin said Wednesday that the military would turn over power to the interim government in about two weeks, after a constitutional drafting committee is appointed. But elections under the new constitution aren't planned until October 2007.  \par \par \uc2 The initial comments were sufficient for the time being to restore confidence, but investors won't want to wait too long to know who will be in the interim government Cabinet.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers expect the dollar is expected to find immediate support at THB37.40, while resistance is pegged at THB37.60. [ 21-09-06 1049GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Thai long-term government bond yields fell as demand from funds returned after an initial bout of panic selling.  \par \par \uc2 Yields were expected to settle around current levels pending news of fourth quarter supply, which could come at any time between now and early October.  \par \par \uc2 However, short- and medium-term yields rose under pressure from offshore and onshore \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap rates. Swap rates were higher on heavy dollar buying after the coup, which raised demand for the baht in the Thai financial system.  \par \par \uc2 The average bid on the government bond due August 2008 rose almost three basis points to 5.084%, the July 2016 bid fell three basis points to 5.270%, and the July 2026 bid was down four basis points to end at 5.422%, according to the Web site of the Thai Bond Market Association.  \par \par \uc2 In early panic selling, the 10-year bond bid had jumped to 5.36% and the 20-year to 5.60%, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The most-active overnight interbank interest rate may have risen to around 4.98% from 4.95% Tuesday due to temporary tight liquidity in the financial system.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Thailand sold at auction for the first time Thursday THB5 billion worth of its three-day bonds, introducing a new tool for control of liquidity. The bonds will be issued and settled on Monday, two days after the auction as usual. The average yield was 4.974%. The accepted yield range was wide at 4.88%-5.00%, but bid coverage was strong at 2.44 times.  \par \par \uc2 In another sign of continuity in the market after the coup, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold THB1 billion of three-year Government Housing Bank bonds at 5.19%, and THB1 billion of nine-year State Railway of Thailand bonds at 5.635%. Both issues are guaranteed by the ministry.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Market      Term            Thursday              Tuesday \par Interbank   Overnight/ \par             Most-Active     4.98000% (Estimate)   4.95000% \par Repurchase  Overnight/Close 4.87500%              4.84375% \par             14-Day/Close    5.00000%              5.00000% \par Bibor       3-Month/11 am   5.34219%              5.33109% \par             6-Month/11 am   5.38375%              5.37750% \par THB Fix     6-Month/11 am   5.44298%              5.43857% \par IRS Fix     2-Year/11 am    5.41%                 5.40% \par             10-Year/11 am   5.71%                 5.69% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Phisanu Phromchanya and Tom Fox, Dow Jones Newswires; 66 2266 0744; djnews.bangkok@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Mary de Wet [ 21-09-06 1056GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1028 1861  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060921e29l000q4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
