\par\par \b \uc2 WSJA(9/21) Thais Face A Tough Test, But Economy May Benefit  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,654 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2006\par 19:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2006, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Sep 20, 2006 (DJCS via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 \f2 (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ASIA)\par    By James Hookway\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK -- Thailand appears set for a long and potentially difficult transition back to democracy after the military leader who ousted Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra said new elections won't be held for more than a year.  \par \par \uc2 But some economic officials -- including U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson and International Monetary Fund Managing Director Rodrigo de Rato -- suggested that the coup, provided it leads quickly to a credible interim government, won't have a significant negative impact on the Thai economy. Indeed, some analysts contend that Mr. Thaksin's removal will break the political impasse that's clouded the country's business outlook during months of political turmoil.  \par \par \uc2 In Thailand's first coup in 15 years, army commander Gen. Sondhi Boonyaratkalin led a swift, bloodless revolt against Mr. Thaksin's government on Tuesday night, while the Thai premier was in New York to attend the United Nations General Assembly.  \par \par \uc2 Yesterday, a statement broadcast on national television said powerful Thai King Bhumibol Adulyadej endorsed Gen. Sondhi as head of the new interim government, a widely expected endorsement that political analysts consider essential for any Thai government to survive.  \par \par \uc2 Western governments -- including those of the U.S., European Union, Australia and New Zealand -- criticized the coup as a setback for democracy in this Southeast Asian country of 65 million that serves as a manufacturing base for many Asian, U.S. and European companies.  \par \par \uc2 But many Thais have welcomed the coup against Mr. Thaksin, a brash telecommunications billionaire who was as disliked by Bangkok's middle class for his autocratic governing style as he is adored by many poor, rural Thais. Yesterday, Thai families gathered around tanks securing government buildings and the army's Bangkok headquarters and posed for photographs. Women offered roses to troops in armored personnel carriers.  \par \par \uc2 Putting Thailand's democracy back in place will be more complicated to execute than Tuesday's coup and is likely to raise an array of questions about the future of Thai political institutions. And the longer it takes for the new regime to meet its pledge to hold new elections, the more that the patience of pro-democracy Thais with the military takeover will be tested, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 At a news conference yesterday, Gen. Sondhi outlined his time frame for returning Thailand to civilian rule, saying he will act as temporary prime minister for just two weeks before handing over power to an interim civilian government. He didn't indicate who might head the interim administration.  \par \par \uc2 Gen. Sondhi said the new civilian government will oversee the task of drafting a new constitution to remodel the political institutions the coup leaders say Mr. Thaksin systematically eroded during his five years in power. That process could take a year, Gen. Sondhi said, adding that the military's end goal is to hold a new general election in October 2007.  \par \par \uc2 Referring to the military, Gen. Sondhi said: "We have two weeks. After two weeks, we step out."  \par \par \uc2 In the meantime, Thailand will remain under martial law until the military deems the country sufficiently stable for it be lifted, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Despite some disruption of manufacturing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  yesterday, the longer-term impact of the coup on businesses and the economy could be relatively mild, economic analysts say. "There has been very little dislocation in the Thai capital markets, no spillover into the global capital markets," said Mr. Paulson, speaking to reporters in Beijing. "It's always very regrettable when you see change take place this way in a democracy . . . but in terms of the economic impact -- and I don't mean to say an adverse development isn't possible -- but if I'm talking about the top five or six things I'm worried about today, that isn't on the list."  \par \par \uc2 Pridiyathorn Devakula, Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, predicted the economy will improve because the military coup will pave the way for a new interim government to be set up quickly. "From an economic point of view, the result (of the coup) should be an improvement -- the economy can now move forward," he told a news conference yesterday.  \par \par \uc2 "One way to look at it is that the coup leads to a political resolution, and the economy could bounce back quickly," concurred James McCormack, Fitch Ratings' Hong Kong-based head of Asian sovereign ratings, in an interview with Dow Jones Newswires. Fitch and Standard & Poor's put the country on negative credit watch following the coup. Both agencies have a triple-B-plus long-term foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sovereign rating.  \par \par \uc2 Mr. McCormack said Fitch is standing by its forecasts for Thailand's gross-domestic-product growth this year and next of 4.3% and 4.6%, respectively. He said the forecasts have already priced in delays in government construction spending and weakening investment sentiment due to the months-long political crisis that preceded the coup. The Thai economy grew 4.5% in 2005. Mr. McCormack said the country's exports, growing at about 16% this year, would be able to help cushion economic fallout from the coup in the short term.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF's Mr. de Rato said yesterday that Thailand's economy is "fundamentally strong," noting that financial-market reactions to the coup have been limited.  \par \par \uc2 Until earlier this year, Mr. Thaksin was one of Thailand's most effective and popular prime ministers. Economists applauded his efforts to generate wealth in rural areas and his desire to improve Thailand's infrastructure so that the country can better compete with its neighbors, particularly China.  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Thaksin's Thai Rak Thai party won strong parliamentary majorities in two national elections in 2001 and 2005, and Mr. Thaksin is still popular in impoverished rural areas for his easy-credit plans and programs to provide low-cost medical care. "How is the military going to explain itself to the 16 million people who voted for Mr. Thaksin?" said Giles Ungpakorn, a political-science professor at Bangkok's Chulalongkorn University.  \par \par \uc2 But despite Mr. Thaksin's skill as a manager, reflected in the way he rose from the ranks of the police to become one of Asia's richest men as head of a telecommunications empire, his critics said he couldn't resist meddling with Thailand's democratic institutions to entrench his power.  \par \par \uc2 His perceived supporters were appointed to the courts and administrative bodies such as the election commission. Within Thailand's civil service, which is traditionally nonpartisan, support for Mr. Thaksin was often seen as a way to a better career. The Thaksin government also cracked down harshly on Thailand's freewheeling news media.  \par \par \uc2 In January, disenchantment with Mr. Thaksin among Bangkok's middle class and traditional political elite came into the open when the premier's family sold its controlling shares in Shin Corp., the telecommunications company he founded, to a group of investors led by Temasek Holdings Pte. Ltd., an investment company owned by the Singapore government. The sale brought the family $1.9 billion, and was structured so that it paid no taxes on the proceeds. Shortly after the sale was announced, tens of thousands of protesters took to the streets in a series of demonstrations, accusing Mr. Thaksin of profiting at the expense of the country.  \par \par \uc2 In response, Mr. Thaksin called a snap election for April to ease the growing pressure on him, but that plan backfired when the main opposition parties boycotted the vote. That left many seats unfilled -- making it impossible to form a parliament under Thai law, while the pace of economic growth slowed as the political crisis deepened.  \par \par \uc2 Then, in May, King Bhumibol made one of his periodic forays into politics by instructing judges to find a way out of the mess. The king has frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during moments of political crisis in \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sometimes implicitly signing off on military coups, as in 1976, and sometimes nudging Thailand toward the restoration of democracy, as he did in 1992.  \par \par \uc2 The judges responded to King Bhumibol's initiative by voiding April's election and agreeing to hear accusations that Mr. Thaksin's political party had broken election rules.  \par \par \uc2 King Bhumibol is viewed by many Thais as a quasidivine figure. It is considered inappropriate for Thais to speak publicly about the king's possible role in politics, and on Monday, Mr. Thaksin denied suggestions that the king was involved in any of the recent political dramas. "One should not bring him into politics," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, in June Mr. Thaksin said a "charismatic person" was out to remove him from his job as prime minister. Mr. Thaksin went on to say that a mysterious figure whom he refused to name was "wielding extraconstitutional force" to push him from office.  \par \par \uc2 Most Thais assumed that to mean the king or his chief lieutenant, Gen. Prem Tinsulanonda. By appearing to attack the monarch and his supporters, Mr. Thaksin escalated the economically damaging political conflict into a contest between Thailand's royal and military traditions, and a new, more modern way of governing a rapidly industrializing country.  \par \par \uc2 King Bhumibol's advisers struck back with a show of force. Donning his military uniform, Gen. Prem toured army camps around the country warning soldiers that their loyalty is to the king, not Mr. Thaksin.  \par \par \uc2 Other people seen as close to the royal palace gave prominent speeches in recent weeks that were interpreted as critical of Mr. Thaksin's push to modernize the Thai economy and open it up to more foreign investment.  \par \par \uc2 Seizing on that sentiment, local shopkeepers across the country have petitioned the king and the government in recent weeks to block big foreign-owned hypermarkets, such as Tesco PLC of the U.K. and Carrefour SA of France, that are sprouting up across Thailand and taking away their business.  \par \par \uc2 (MORE TO FOLLOW) Dow Jones Newswires  \par \par \uc2 09-20-06 1731ET  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020060920e29k002eg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
