\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Creeping higher as oil, dollar retreat  \b0\par\par\uc2 697 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 August 2006\par 04:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Aug 29 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  crept up on Tuesday, supported by a sharp overnight fall in oil prices and a retreat in the dollar before the release later in the day of minutes from the Federal Reserve's Aug. 8 meeting.  \par \par \uc2 Oil prices fell almost 3 percent on Monday as tropical storm Ernesto was expected to miss U.S. oil installations in the Gulf of Mexico.  \par \par \uc2 That provided a boost to Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which have been hurt in recent sessions by weakness in the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Still, dealers said trade should remain subdued ahead of the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) minutes, which may provide fresh clues on the U.S. interest rate outlook.  \par \par \uc2 "The fall in oil prices to about $70 provided relief to the U.S. stock market, which is welcomed by Asian counterparts," said DBS Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Philip Wee.  \par \par \uc2 "More importantly, the market is looking at tonight's FOMC minutes to close the perception gap between the overly bearish market and more sanguine policymakers regarding the health of the U.S. economy."  \par \par \uc2 At its August meeting, the Fed left interest rates steady after two years of tightening monetary policy to curb inflation.  \par \par \uc2 The South Korean won <KRW=> firmed a third of a percent to about 961.30 per U.S. dollar, while the Taiwan dollar <TWD=> rose to around 32.86 to the U.S. dollar, up almost 0.3 percent from Monday's five-week trough at about 32.96.  \par \par \uc2 Wee said foreign capital flows out of Korean shares were one reason why the Taiwan dollar was likely to perform better than the won.  \par \par \uc2 From a regional perspective, Taiwan's equity market has attracted the most net foreign buying in the year to date, while South Korea has seen the most foreign selling.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> firmed to 1.5750 per U.S. dollar, up from Monday's two-week low at 1.5815, while the Philippine peso <PHP=> hit a one-week high of 51.15 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The Thai baht <THB=> also rose to its highest in about a week, at around 37.60 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the baht was bolstered by news late on Monday that GE Money, the consumer finance unit of General Electric Co. , had agreed to acquire a 25.4 percent stake in Thailand's Bank of Ayudhya Ltd. for $600 million.  \par \par \uc2 The deal would give the U.S. conglomerate a foothold in Thailand's retail banking business.  \par \par \uc2 ABN AMRO \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Shahab Jalinoos said in a note that the market had been looking to push the dollar up because of political problems in \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold the baht down, but the merger news "could spur a near-term correction lower back towards 37.50". \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0206 GMT   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       116.82         117.00       +0.15\par  Sing dlr        1.5753         1.5766       +0.08\par  Taiwan dlr      32.860         32.918       +0.18\par  Korean won      961.00         964.00       +0.31\par  Baht             37.61          37.67       +0.16\par  Peso             51.16          51.21       +0.10\par  Rupiah         9111.00        9125.00       +0.15\par  Ringgit         3.6755         3.6790       +0.10\par  Yuan            7.9652         7.9707       +0.07\