\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Pare losses, oil worries remain  \b0\par\par\uc2 765 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2006\par 05:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Aug 22 (Reuters) - Gains in regional stock markets helped Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  trim losses on Tuesday but worries over high oil prices remained after Iran, the world's fourth-largest oil exporter, said it would press on with its nuclear programme.  \par \par \uc2 "Equities is one reason," said a trader in Singapore, referring to a recovery in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "There is also a lot of options-related trade going on today so that has been driving the market."  \par \par \uc2 The yen, which was weaker both against the U.S. dollar and the euro <EURJPY=>, also set the tone for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was undermined by concerns over its low yields and widening interest rate differentials.  \par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to raise rates again after bringing rates up to 3 percent earlier this month, while the Bank of Japan is likely to lift rates only gradually after a rise to 0.25 percent last month, its first increase in six years.  \par \par \uc2 Oil traded above $72 per barrel on Tuesday after rising more than $1 on Monday. Iran is expected to respond on Tuesday to a package of incentives from the six world powers that aims to end a nuclear stand-off.  \par \par \uc2 Its commitment to its nuclear programme raised the prospect of U.N. sanctions being imposed on the country and of Iran using oil as a weapon in the row.  \par \par \uc2 "Oil prices are up again. It's quite uncertain so it's being a drag on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Irene Cheung, a regional economist at ABN AMRO Bank.  \par \par \uc2 Apart from the yen factor, a dealer at a commercial bank in Thailand said the Thai baht <THB=> tested the downside after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week in the range of 37.40-37.45 per dollar to slow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise.  \par \par \uc2 The baht has been the strongest in the region this year, driven by foreign portfolio inflows, a strengthening current account and, more recently, signs of a resolution to the country's political stalemate.  \par \par \uc2 The market was looking forward to news by Thursday of the appointment of members of a new election commission that will pave the way for a general election on Oct. 15.  \par \par \uc2 "Failure to do so would risk a postponement of the October 15 election date, setting Thailand off its course to expeditiously settle the already long-drawn-out political standstill," UBS said in a note.  \par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> recovered after being down most of the day.  \par \par \uc2 "The stock market was up, volume was down but foreigners were selling, not buying any more," ABN AMRO's Cheung said.  \par \par \uc2 Taiwan had net equity outflows of $16.6 million for the first time in more than two weeks, stock exchange data showed.  \par \par \uc2 Nomura International said foreign investors were net buyers of $1.4 billion of Asian equities last week, the highest in 15 weeks. Only South Korea and the Philippines recorded net outflows. Foreign investors have been net sellers in South Korea for six weeks in a row, Nomura said.  \par \par \uc2 Cheung said the Taiwan dollar, which reached a one-week low of 32.774 per U.S. dollar, would remain pressured by local political problems as a campaign to oust Taiwan President Chen Shui-bian gathers steam.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at 0814 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \