\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Thai  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes \highlight\uc2  to slow baht rise  \b0\par\par\uc2 222 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2006\par 12:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK, Aug 10 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the baht's rise against the dollar on Thursday, but does not expect the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to return to levels of 38 to 39 per dollar, governor Pridiyathorn Devakula said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action was intended mainly to smooth out baht volatility without trying to resist the broad market trend, he said.  \par \par \uc2 "We have entered the market today to try to ease its volatility. But at the same time, we need to look at the global picture," Pridiyathorn told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 Asked if the baht might soon return to the 38 per dollar level last seen on July 26 or the 39 to the dollar last hit on March 24, he said: "That is wishful thinking."  \par \par \uc2 The baht, the best performing Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, rose about 0.49 percent in late trade to around 37.43 per dollar. It has strengthened about 9.85 percent this year.  \par \par \uc2 It's sustained strength since early this year did not seem to have hurt Thai exports which increased 17.1 percent during the first half of 2006 compared with the same period of 2005, Pridiyathorn said.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-THAILAND-BAHT (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060810e28a0014q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
