\par\par \b \uc2 Thai baht retreats as poll verdict, oil weigh  \b0\par\par\uc2 367 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Mai 2006\par 09:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, May 2 (Reuters) - The Thai baht retreated on Tuesday from a six-year high hit the previous day as some traders expected the country's Constitutional court to scrap April's inconclusive general election, prolonging the political crisis.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also weighed down by concerns about the impact of soaring oil prices on domestic consumption and inflation. The government said on Monday annual consumer price inflation accelerated unexpectedly in April from March, putting further pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise rates.  \par \par \uc2 The baht <THB=> weakened to the day's low of 37.69 per dollar, down half a percent from Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Financial markets were closed on Monday when the Constitutional Court agreed to hear a petition by law lecturers and a civic group to annul the April 2 general election.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the political uncertainty, near-record high oil prices, rising inflation and a slowing economy all combined to encourage traders to bid for dollars. The dollar's rebound against the yen also helped push up the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the baht, they said.  \par \par \uc2 "The economy is now calming down because inflation is higher due to oil prices. People are not willing to invest much," said a Bangkok-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Bank of Thailand was seen buying dollars last week to stifle the baht's gains despite the worsening outlook for the economy.  \par \par \uc2 The baht has climbed 9 percent this year, making it the second best performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia after the Indonesian rupiah <IDR=>.  \par \par \uc2 IDEAGlobal said in a report it expected Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  to step up its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the baht's gains, given the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strong performance this year. It pointed to the latest reserves data, which it said indicated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves rose by $2 billion to $56.6 billion between March 24 and April 21, according to the latest data disclosed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ((Editing by Alex Richardson; rajat.bhattacharya@reuters.com; Reuters Messaging: rajat.bhattacharya.reuters.com@reuters.net; +65 6870 3841))  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-THAILAND-BAHT|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SI|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060502e252000tx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
