\par\par \b \uc2 Thai Baht Ends Down Tad On Offshore Dlr Buying;Bonds Up  \b0\par\par\uc2 575 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2006\par 13:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The Thai baht ended slightly lower against the U.S. dollar Tuesday on offshore dollar buying in late trade after suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices closed higher, recovering some recent losses after an auction showed that demand for long-term issues remains strong amid moderate supply.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at THB37.98, up from THB37.93 at Monday's close in Asia.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. unit eased to THB37.89 in the morning, in line with its regional trend, after rising against the trend overnight to a high of THB38.09.  \par \par \uc2 Some traders suspected that the Bank of Thailand was behind the dollar's gains overnight, ahead of a meeting Tuesday with caretaker Finance Minister Thanong Bidaya, who had expressed concern about the possible effect of the baht's gains on exports.  \par \par \uc2 However, Thanong said after meeting \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials Tuesday that he wasn't worried about the baht's strength, as the local unit has been supported by capital inflows into Thai assets.  \par \par \uc2 Caretaker Commerce Minister Somkid Jatusripitak said late Monday that March exports likely exceeded $10 billion, after rising 23.3% on year to $9.43 billion in February.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said a mild recovery by the dollar against other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday afternoon prompted buying on weakness against the baht by foreign players, but the dollar's rise was capped at an intraday high of THB37.99 due to selling by local exporters.  \par \par \uc2 Traders expect the dollar to hover in the THB37.90-THB38.00 range overnight and in offshore trade Wednesday. Thai financial markets will be closed Wednesday for a public holiday. [ 18-04-06 1120GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Government bond yields ended slightly lower in a mild correction after Tuesday's auction showed that demand still outstrips supply, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The bias for yields remains upward, in line with the trend in interest rates, but dealers generally agreed with Thanong, who said Tuesday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 14-day repurchase market target rate is likely near its peak after last week's 25-basis point hike to 4.75%.  \par \par \uc2 The average bid for the government bond due August 2008 was down marginally at 5.499%, the December 2014 bid eased three basis points to 5.826%, the July 2016 bid dropped two basis points to 5.871% and the bid on the March 2021 issue ended almost unchanged at 6.193%, according to the Thai Bond Market Association's Web site.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Thailand sold at auction Tuesday THB4 billion of government bonds due July 2016 at an average yield of 5.8954% with strong bid cover of 3.47 times. Last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  auctioned THB2.5 billion of March 2021 bonds at an average 6.0907% with bid cover of 2.58 times.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight money market interest rate was expected to have remained around 4.63%.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Market     Term            Tuesday                Monday \par Interbank  Overnight/ \par            Most-Active     4.63000% (Estimate)    4.63000% \par Repurchase Overnight/Close 4.62500%               4.62500% \par            14-Day/Close    4.75000%               4.75000% \par Bibor      3-Month/11 am   5.15922%               5.15078% \par            6-Month/11 am   5.31969%               5.30953% \par THB Fix    6-Month/11 am   5.60313%               5.61520% \par    \f28 \pa