\par\par \b \uc2 Thai Baht Ends Off High On Possible  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 ;Bonds Dn  \b0\par\par\uc2 758 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2006\par 12:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The Thai baht was higher against the dollar late Tuesday on gains in some regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and equity-related inflows, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 However, the baht's rise was apparently capped by continued dollar buying by the Bank of Thailand, traders suspected.  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices were quoted lower in line with the rising interest rate trend, but trading was razor-thin as most players were on the sidelines ahead of a long holiday for Thailand's traditional new year.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at THB38.03, down from THB38.14 at Monday's close in Asia. The U.S. unit spiraled down to a fresh six-year low of THB37.92 before noon. The intraday low was the lowest since April 2000, beating Monday's record of THB37.98.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. unit clung around its intraday high of THB38.05 in the morning, then continued its retreat.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar's slide was more in line with its losses against the yuan and the Singapore dollar than the dollar-yen, while the (equity) inflows remained supportive to the baht," said a local trader.  \par \par \uc2 Soon after it dived to the intraday low, the dollar bounced back.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have stepped in around THB37.95, although its authorities avoided mentioning any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when asked by reporters.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  Deputy Governor Bandid Nijathaworn said that the Thai baht's current strength was mainly due to steady capital inflows.  \par \par \uc2 However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't determined whether those inflows are for investment or for speculation, Bandid told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 "We will monitor the market closely to make sure that the baht is not too strong," he said, but added that his remarks weren't meant to suggest the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market at the moment.  \par \par \uc2 Bandid's remark followed comments by Bank of Thailand Governor Pridayathorn Devakula, who said earlier Tuesday that the baht exchange rate moved in line with regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Traders expect that the dollar's support at THB37.95 should hold Wednesday morning, with resistance at THB38.10. [ 11-04-06 1022GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Government bond yields ended higher in a wide bid-offer range amid very thin trade due to upcoming long holidays and a large number of auctions in the primary market, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Thai financial markets will be closed on Thursday and Friday for public holidays.  \par \par \uc2 "There were few deals done as most investors paid attention to the primary market rather than the secondary market," said a local bank trader.  \par \par \uc2 There were three auctions on Tuesday - state enterprise bonds, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds and government bonds - with a combined value of around THB16 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold THB500 million of eight-year Expressway & Rapid Transit Authority, or ETA, bonds at auction Tuesday with an accepted yield of 5.83%, as well as THB1 billion of nine-year ETA bonds at 5.89%.  \par \par \uc2 The accepted yields represented premiums of around 14-17 basis points over the government bond yield curve. Despite the government guarantee, the premiums were wider than normal, which is around 10-12 basis points, due to the long maturity.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Thailand sold THB12 billion worth of its 363-day \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds in an auction at 5.29190%, up from 5.19392% at last week's auction.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also sold THB2.5 billion of government bonds due March 2021 at 6.0907%, with a bid coverage ratio of 2.58 times.  \par \par \uc2 The average bid for government bonds due August 2008 was up four basis points at 5.436%, while the bid on the July 2013 issue rose five basis points to 5.695%. The July 2016 bid was up six basis points to 5.808%, and the bid on the March 2021 issue was up seven basis points to end at 6.130%, according to Thai Bond Market Association's Web site.  \par \par \uc2 The most active overnight interbank rate rose slightly due to tight liquidity ahead of the long holidays.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Market     Term            Tuesday                Monday \par Interbank  Overnight/ \par            Most-Active     4.70000% (Estimated)   4.63000% \par Repurchase Overnight/Close 4.62500%               4.62500% \par            14-Day/Close    4.75000%               4.50000% \par Bibor      3-Month/11 am   5.11100%               5.09328% \par            6-Month/11 am   5.28225%               5.26375% \par THB Fix    6-Month/11 am   5.58891%               5.55561% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ratthapol Onsanit and Suttinee Yuvejwattana, Dow Jones Newswires; 66 2266 0744; djnews.bangkok@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Graham Norris [ 11-04-06 1039GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1028 1861  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060411e24b000hx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
