\par\par \b \uc2 Thai Ctrl Bk Hasn't  \highlight22\uc2 Intervened \highlight\uc2  On Baht Strength-Gov  \b0\par\par\uc2 213 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2006\par 11:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the recent strength of the baht against the U.S. dollar, Bank of Thailand Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula said late Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "The baht strength is due to investor confidence (in the Thai economy)," Pridiyathorn told reporters. [ 07-04-06 0944GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Some traders suspected that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped into the market Friday to slow the baht's gains.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to its intraday low of THB38.06, the lowest intraday level since May 2000, before quickly bouncing back in the late afternoon.  \par \par \uc2 At 0950 GMT, the dollar was quoted at THB38.155, down from THB38.29 in the early morning and THB38.39 at Wednesday's close in Asia on hefty equity-related inflows following easing political concerns after Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra decided Tuesday to step down. Thai markets were closed Thursday for a public holiday.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Thanong Bidaya said early Friday that the baht isn't too strong, and is moving in line with broad weakness of the U.S. dollar against other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 -By Bangkok Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; 662-266-0744; djnews.bangkok@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Hilary Mc Cully [ 07-04-06 1013GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060407e247000mg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
