\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Thai Baht Ends Up On Inflows But Off 6-Yr High; Bonds Dn  \b0\par\par\uc2 440 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2006\par 14:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2006, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The Thai baht rallied further Friday on continued portfolio inflows amid easing political tension following Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra's decision to step down, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 They said they suspected that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  brought the baht off a fresh six-year high but Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula denied the authority had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices edged lower in cautious trade ahead of Monday's monetary policy committee meeting, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 14-day repurchase market target rate is widely expected to rise 25 basis points to 4.75%.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at THB38.19, down from THB38.39 at Wednesday's close in Asia. Thai financial markets were closed Thursday for a public holiday. The U.S. unit plunged mid-afternoon to a fresh six-year low of THB38.06, the lowest level since May 2000.  \par \par \uc2 Thaksin said Tuesday that he wouldn't take the prime minister's post in the next government, likely to be formed around the end of the month following snap elections on April 2.  \par \par \uc2 'The baht was boosted by portfolio inflows. Easing worries on the political front are making foreign investors return to Thailand, at least for the time being,' said a dealer at a local bank.  \par \par \uc2 The Stock Exchange of Thailand index ended up 3.1% Wednesday, with foreign investors buying a net THB15 billion.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was also weighed down by exporter sales amid fears that the U.S. unit could fall further, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The baht's rise was also supported by Thai Finance Minister Thanong Bidaya's comment Friday that a dollar rate of around THB38.00 is in line with regional units and not considered too strong. However, Thanong said any further slump in the dollar could pose a serious concern, citing an analyst's forecast suggesting the dollar may fall by another 30% against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  given the fundamentals of the U.S. economy.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the dollar's fall was limited by suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 'It seems very clear that the authority has stepped in. Without such a move, the dollar would have dropped more,' said another dealer.  \par \par \uc2 However, the Bank of Thailand's Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula said late Friday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hadn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the recent strength of the baht.  \par \par \uc2 'The baht strength is due to investor confidence (in the Thai economy),' he told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 Traders expect the dollar to test firm psychological support at THB38.00 next week, while initial resistance is pegged at THB38.30.  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020060407e247008vi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
