\par\par \b \uc2 Thai Baht Ends Up As PM Decides To Step Down; Bonds Mixed  \b0\par\par\uc2 710 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2006\par 12:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The Thai baht finished higher against the U.S. dollar Wednesday, as Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra's decision to step down appeared to break the political impasse that has haunted the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for months, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 However, the baht ended off a one-year high on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the dollar's intraday rebound against other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they said.  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices ended mixed as the yield curve continued to flatten under pressure from expectations the Bank of Thailand will on Monday raise its 14-day repurchase market target rate by 25 basis points to 4.75%.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at THB38.39, down from THB38.64 at Tuesday's close in Asia.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. unit plunged to a one-year low of THB38.25 in the morning after Thaksin said Tuesday night he wouldn't take the prime minister's post in the next government, likely to be formed around the end of the month following snap elections on April 2.  \par \par \uc2 Organizers of anti-Thaksin protests and key opposition parties said they generally accepted Thaksin's decision.  \par \par \uc2 "Thaksin's move lifted huge pressure on the baht and triggered heavy baht-buying by local and foreign players," said a local trader.  \par \par \uc2 Strong foreign buying in Thai shares also hammered the dollar, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The Stock Exchange of Thailand index ended up 3.1% Wednesday on a huge trading value of THB51.07 billion.  \par \par \uc2 But the U.S. unit proved resilient. It bounced back against regional units, pulling the dollar-baht off lows.  \par \par \uc2 "There was dollar short-covering in the afternoon in line with the its movement against some Asian units," said another trader.  \par \par \uc2 Traders also said the Bank of Thailand might have stepped in to stem the baht's surge in the afternoon, fueling the dollar's rebound.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seemed to have come in at THB38.30, they said.  \par \par \uc2 Aside from concern about the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , investors may have also begun to consider the various unknowns in Thaksin's plan. By-elections for seats left unfilled in Sunday's elections could delay the opening of the legislature, and if Thaksin tries to assume an active role in the next government outside of the premiership, mass protests against him may continue.  \par \par \uc2 Traders expected the dollar to swing in the THB38.25-THB38.48 range overnight, subject to a rate decision overnight by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its impact on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Thai markets are closed Thursday for a public holiday. [ 05-04-06 1043GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Government bond yields ended mixed, as the long end of the curve has apparently priced in the expected interest rate increase, while medium-term yields continued to edge higher, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The Thai Bond Market Association said the average bid yield on the government bond due November 2009 was up two basis points to 5.463%, and the bid on the March 2012 bond added one basis point to 5.543%. But the July 2013 bid eased one basis point to 5.613%, the July 2016 bid dropped one basis point to 5.717%, and the bid on the new March 2021 issue slipped two basis points to end at 6.012%.  \par \par \uc2 Political uncertainty had a very limited effect on the market, as movement has been dominated by interest rate expectations, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The most-active overnight interbank rate was expected to have remained around 4.39%-4.40%, with a downward bias after slipping to 4.39% Tuesday. Banks have been preparing cash for extra demand over several holidays this month and for investment Monday at the higher rate expected in the 14-day repurchase market term.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Market     Term            Wednesday              Tuesday \par Interbank  Overnight/ \par            Most-Active     4.39%-4.40%(Estimated) 4.39000% \par Repurchase Overnight/Close 4.34375%               4.34375% \par            14-Day/Close    4.50000%               4.50000% \par Bibor      3-Month/11 am   5.05953%               5.05078% \par            6-Month/11 am   5.22594%               5.21094% \par THB Fix    6-Month/11 am   5.50474%               5.52283% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Rattaphol Onsanit and Tom Fox, Dow Jones Newswires; 66 2266 0744; djnews.bangkok@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -Edited by Graham Norris \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 05-04-06 1050GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1028 1861  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060405e245000i0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
