\par\par \b \uc2 Thai Baht Ends Dn On Political Worries; Bonds Higher  \b0\par\par\uc2 860 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 2006\par 12:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The Thai baht ended lower against the U.S. dollar in thin Asian spot trade Monday due to offshore dollar buying prompted by concerns over the stability of the Thai government, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Long-term government bond prices rose following developments which took place Friday, including a rise in U.S.Treasurys and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying in the Thai market.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at THB39.36, up from THB39.29 at Friday's Asian close.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. unit traded early Monday around the intraday low of THB39.18, tracking its weakness against other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Foreigners began to pick up the dollar, however, after they were spooked by escalating political concerns.  \par \par \uc2 Efforts to oust Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra gained momentum after his influential political mentor, Chamlong Srimuang, called for him to quit and announced he would join a mass anti-Thaksin protest Sunday.  \par \par \uc2 Thaksin called an emergency Cabinet meeting Sunday night after Chamlong's announcement and told his ministers "he will not resign, but if the situation deteriorates, he will dissolve the parliament," said government spokesman Suraphong Suebwonglee.  \par \par \uc2 For the time being, the threat to dissolve the parliament appears to be a last resort, and a reminder to those opposed to Thaksin that his Thai Rak Thai Party would likely return to power with strong rural support. A move to dissolve the house would lead to new elections.  \par \par \uc2 The jitters were expected to continue weighing on the market while investors wait and see the strength and strategies of anti-Thaksin protesters at Sunday's rally.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar climbed to an intraday high of THB39.37 in the afternoon and remained near that level for the remainder of the Asian day.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Thailand's January customs-based trade data showed a higher deficit due to seasonal weakness in exports. The Ministry of Commerce said Monday that based on raw customs department data, the deficit in the country's balance of trade rose to $442 million in January from $142.1 million in December.  \par \par \uc2 But January import growth was at its lowest level in 43 months - at 2% on year - due mostly to lower oil imports after the elimination of a subsidy on retail prices in July of last year, and government pressure on the private sector to limit imports of oil, gold and steel.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. unit is expected to trade overnight in a range of THB39.20-THB39.50, traders said. [ 20-02-06 1120GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Most Thai long-term government bond yields ended down in line with movement in U.S. Treasury yields Friday, based on expectations that further U.S. interest rate increases will help to keep inflation under control in the long term, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Sentiment was supported by Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market Friday, when it bought maturies of seven years and up, along with limited local supply of long-term issues, they said.  \par \par \uc2 The Thai Bond Market Association said the average bid yield on the government bond due August 2008 was nearly unchanged at 4.990%. But the July 2013 bid was down almost three basis points at 5.274%, the July 2016 bid fell four basis points to 5.483%, and the August 2019 bid dropped three basis points to end at 5.690%.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Thailand sold at auction Monday THB19 billion worth of treasury bills at yields mostly higher than at the previous auction.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold THB5 billion worth of 28-day t-bills at an average 3.77429%, up from 3.76371% at the last auction on Feb. 10. Another THB7 billion of 91-day t-bills was sold at an average 3.96044%, up from 3.94360%.  \par \par \uc2 But THB7 billion worth of 182-day t-bills was sold at an average 4.13235%, down from THB4.14689% previously due to demand from short-term funds set up to provide alternatives to low-interest rates offered on bank deposit accounts.  \par \par \uc2 The auction results triggered a downward correction in secondary market t-bill bid yields, which had risen recently due to expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise its 14-day repurchase market target rate by 25 basis points to 4.5% at its next policy meeting on March 8.  \par \par \uc2 The bid on 28-day t-bills was down seven basis points to an average 3.836%, while the 91-day bid dropped two basis points to 3.983%, and the 182-day bid eased one basis point to 4.143%, the Thai Bond Market Association said.  \par \par \uc2 The most-active overnight interbank rate was expected to have remained unchanged at 4.14%.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Market     Term            Monday                 Friday \par Interbank  Overnight/ \par            Most-Active     4.14000% (Estimated)   4.14000% \par Repurchase Overnight/Close 4.09375%               4.09375% \par            14-Day/Close    4.25000%               4.25000% \par Bibor      3-Month/11 am   4.69156%               4.68750% \par            6-Month/11 am   4.90000%               4.88938% \par THB Fix    6-Month/11 am   5.14235%               5.14142% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Rattaphol Onsanit and Tom Fox, Dow Jones Newswires; 662 266 0744; djnews.bangkok@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Hilary Mc Cully [ 20-02-06 1127GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1028 1861  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060220e22k000iq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pn