\par\par \b \uc2 PRESS DIGEST - Thai newspapers - January 6  \b0\par\par\uc2 391 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 2006\par 02:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, Jan 6 (Reuters) - These are some of the leading stories in Thai newspapers on Friday. Reuters has not verified these stories and does not vouch for their accuracy.  \par \par \uc2  TOP STORIES  \par \par \uc2  - Police detained four men for questioning after taking DNA samples from them as part of the investigation into the murder of Welsh university student Katherine Horton on the resort island of Samui (THE NATION)  \par \par \uc2  - The sixth round of free trade talks with the United States in the northern city of Chiang Mai next week will face the wrath of about 10,000 protesters from 12 special interest groups which have vowed to rally throughout the five-day meeting (THE NATION)  \par \par \uc2  BUSINESS  \par \par \uc2  - About 400 captains of Thai industry will be reassured during a meeting with Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra next Wednesday that any effects on the economy of the weakening U.S. dollar will be minimal, a Commerce Ministry source said (THE NATION)  \par \par \uc2  - Thai Beverage PCL, Thailand's largest brewer, plans to seek formal permission from the Securities and Exchange Commission early next week to list its stock on the Singapore Exchange after the company's board approved the dual-listing plan (THE NATION)  \par \par \uc2  - Economic growth this year will probably outpace last year's because the worst of a slew of negative factors has passed, according to the Commerce Ministry (THE NATION)  \par \par \uc2  - The pace of Thailand's year-on-year inflation eased slightly in December, but not enough to change expectations for further interest rate increases by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BUSINESS DAY)  \par \par \uc2  - Thailand, the world's fourth biggest sugar supplier, asked producers and distributors of sweetener to report stockpiles of 10,000 kg or more to curb hoarding of the commodity amid a severe shortage (BUSINESS DAY)  \par \par \uc2  - Consumer spending power looks to shrink this year with eggs, petrol gas and sugar, traditional key cost-of-living benchmarks, costing more while the government comes under fire for being superficial in its efforts to ease the strain (BANGKOK POST)  \par \par \uc2  - Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent the baht from rising (BUSINESS DAY)  \par \par \uc2  ** Looking for more information from local sources? Factiva.com has eight Thai sources including the Bangkok Post and Business Day.    \par \par \uc2 THAILAND-PRESS|LANGEN|G|RBN|REVU|RNP|PGE|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060106e2160002j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
