\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Led higher by peso, but Korean won stumbles  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Dhara Ranasinghe  \par \uc2 804 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2005\par 05:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Oct 21 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened against a broadly weak dollar on Friday, with the peso receiving an extra boost from the previous day's unexpected rise in Philippine interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 The South Korean won <KRW=><KRW=KFTC> was the exception. It weakened almost a third of a percent to a nine-month low at 1,058.8 per dollar as foreign investors continued to bail out of the local equity market.  \par \par \uc2 South Korean shares <.KS11> closed slightly higher on Friday, but foreign investors were net sellers for the 21st consecutive trading session, dumping a net 238 billion won in stocks on the day.  \par \par \uc2 In contrast to the won, the peso <PHP=> rose half a percent to about 55.40 per dollar -- a three-month high.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said Thursday's 25 basis-point rise in Philippine interest rates and a move through key chart barriers boosted the peso.  \par \par \uc2 "The peso opened at 55.61 (per dollar) and that is due to the fact that rates were raised," said a trader in Manila. "We're looking for another hike in November or December, especially with the U.S. likely to hike rates again."  \par \par \uc2 Strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows from Filipinos working overseas together with higher interest rates were expected to bolster the peso heading into the end of the year.  \par \par \uc2 The country's top court also cleared the way this week for the government to expand a sales tax, the centre piece of fiscal reform.  \par \par \uc2 "We've been turning bullish on the peso in the last few weeks," said Philip Wee, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at DBS Bank. "Remittances are one factor and the dollar has been unable to keep rallying."  \par \par \uc2 YEN HELP  \par \par \uc2 Sentiment towards Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  picked up as the yen bounced back from this week's two-year low against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , along side the yen <JPY=>, have been hurt this month by expectations that U.S. interest rates will keep rising to contain inflation.  \par \par \uc2 But the dollar succumbed to profit taking on Friday, allowing regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to recover.  \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. dollar is weaker across the board, but the market is still trying to shape its opinion on U.S. growth," said BNP Paribas senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Thio Chin Loo.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> added about a quarter of a percent to 1.6903 per dollar, moving further away from a three-month low hit on Wednesday at about 1.6990.  \par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> was a touch firmer at about 33.63 per U.S. dollar, above Wednesday's one-year low near 33.73, while the Indian rupee <INR=IN> recovered from this week's 11-month lows against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers in Bangkok said they suspected the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the baht's gains following its decision earlier this week to raise interest rates for the eighth time since last August.  \par \par \uc2 The baht was little changed at about 40.86 per dollar <THB=> and underperforming most of its regional peers.  \par \par \uc2 A trader said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was worried the baht could strengthen further, especially with the yen's rebound dragging other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with it.  \par \par \uc2 "They may have come in this morning at around 40.85 as the yen rebounded against the dollar," the trader said.  \par \par \uc2 Aggressive monetary tightening in Thailand has boosted the baht, but analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not want to see too strong a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as this would hurt exporter competitiveness. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0837 GMT   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       115.21         115.45       +0.21\par  Sing dlr        1.6920         1.6944       +0.14\par  Taiwan dlr      33.632         33.681       +0.15\par  Korean won     1057.80        1055.90       -0.18\par  Baht             40.86          40.85       -0.02\par  Peso             55.39          55.71       +0.58\par  Rupiah        10058.00       10080.00       +0.22\par  Rupee            45.04          45.22       +0.40\par  Ringgit         3.7743         3.7745       +0.01\par  Yuan            8.0885         8.0905       +0.02\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       115.21         102.45      -11.08\par  Sing dlr        1.6920         1.6310       -3.61\par  Taiwan dlr      33.632         31.920       -5.09\par  Korean won     1057.80        1035.10       -2.15\par  Baht             40.86          38.88       -4.85\par  Peso             55.39          56.28       +1.61\par  Rupiah        10058.00        9290.00       -7.64\par  Rupee            45.04          43.47       -3.49\par  Ringgit         3.7743         3.8000       +0.68\par  Yuan            8.0885         8.2760       +2.32\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SI|Z|IF|PHD|J|SNS|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD  