\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Baht hits 6-week high, Sing dlr strengthens  \b0\par\par\uc2 829 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2005\par 06:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Aug 10 (Reuters) - The Thai baht hit its strongest level against the dollar in more than six weeks on Wednesday, with the return of investors to the local stock market helping it outperform other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which were largely steady.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> gained as much as a quarter of a percent to 1.6483 per dollar after data showing a higher-than-expected, annualised 18 percent jump in gross domestic product in the second quarter from the first.  \par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> gained a fifth of a percent to 9,780 per dollar a day after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  surprised the market by raising a key rate by a quarter of a point to 8.75 percent in a bid to fight inflation and support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were little changed from late Asian trading on Tuesday after the U.S. Federal Reserve's 10th consecutive rate increase and its promise to continue tightening policy helped support the dollar. The baht <THB=> added half a percent to 40.99 per dollar, its best showing since June 24. It has rebounded about 2.5 percent since it hit its weakest level for more than two years on July 8.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said share price gains and expectations that Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will strengthen following last month's revaluation of the Chinese yuan had helped the baht's recovery.  \par \par \uc2 "There has been a mini-transfer of hot money into Thailand recently. Stocks are doing well and this has lifted the baht," said a trader at a local bank in Bangkok.  \par \par \uc2 IDEAglobal.com said in a note to clients that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the baht rising. It said any further gains in the yen, which the baht tracks closely, could boost the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 40.80 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE DOLLAR  \par \par \uc2 Khor Hoe Ee, assistant managing director of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS), said the policy of allowing a gradual appreciation in the Singapore dollar was appropriate as inflation was likely to pick up in the second half.  \par \par \uc2 Khor also said that a stronger Singapore dollar, which was trading within the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's preferred range, would not affect the city-state's export outlook.  \par \par \uc2 IDEAglobal said that although a strong run of economic data had bolstered the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strengthening bias for the Singapore dollar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was already near the stronger end of MAS's preferred trading range, making it difficult for traders to push it higher.  \par \par \uc2 It said the U.S. dollar was likely to find support at 1.6475/80 Singapore dollars on Wednesday. The 1.6520 level was the first level of resistance if the Singapore dollar weakened, IDEAglobal said.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar hit a two-month high of 1.6465 per dollar on Aug. 4. It has lost 1 percent against the dollar this year, compared with a 7.8 percent depreciation in the Japanese yen <JPY=>.  \par \par \uc2 The Singaporean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducts monetary policy by guiding the Singapore dollar against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its major trading partners. It does not disclose the composition of the trade-weighted basket or its preferred trading range.  \par \par \uc2 "The comments from the MAS suggest it is more comfortable with the global outlook and that there is not too much implication for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Philip Wee, a DBS Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, said. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par at   0800 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       111.13         112.03       +0.81\par  Sing dlr        1.6478         1.6527       +0.30\par  Taiwan dlr      31.922         31.963       +0.13\par  Korean won     1014.60        1013.00       -0.16\par  Baht             41.00          41.20       +0.49\par  Peso             55.85          55.88       +0.05\par  Rupiah         9775.00        9797.00       +0.23\par  Rupee            43.54          43.54       +0.01\par  Ringgit         3.7475         3.7487       +0.03\par  Yuan            8.1062         8.1070       +0.01 Change on\par year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       111.13         102.45       -7.81\par  Sing dlr        1.6478         1.6310       -1.02\par  Taiwan dlr      31.922         31.920       -0.01\par  Korean won     1014.60        1035.10       +2.02\par  Baht             41.00          38.88       -5.17\par  Peso             55.85          56.28       +0.78\par  Rupiah         9775.00        9290.00       -4.96\par  Rupee            43.54          43.47       -0.15\par  Ringgit         3.7475         3.8000       +1.40\par  Yuan            8.1062         8.2760       +2.09 ((Reporting\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 by Rajat Bhattacharya and Dhara Ranasinghe; editing by Alan Raybould; rajat.bhattacharya@reuters.com; Reuters Messaging: rajat.bhattacharya.reute