\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Thai Ctrl Banker: Yuan Change Good For China, World, Baht  \b0\par\par\uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juli 2005\par 17:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula said late Thursday that China's switch to pegging the yuan to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , instead of solely to the U.S. dollar, will be good for China, good for the global economy and good for the Thai baht.  \par \par \uc2 Pridiyathorn told a press conference he is pleased with the change because it will help stabilize regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and facilitate trade.  \par \par \uc2 Now that the uncertainty has ended, he said: 'the baht should be less volatile.'  \par \par \uc2 The baht and other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have fluctuated in recent months as investors speculated about the timing of China's move, appreciating in anticipation of a yuan revaluation when they thought the time was near, and depreciating when it failed to happen.  \par \par \uc2 The baht strengthened by 1.27% from THB41.79 to the dollar to THB41.26 in the first two hours after the Bank of China announced the change, Pridiyathorn said.  \par \par \uc2 The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't interfere with the post-yuan revaluation volatility because it was normal movement in line with strengthening Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said.  \par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue monitoring the baht's movements closely, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a long standing policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit daily volatility and keep the baht in line with regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , without trying to fight market trends.  \par \par \uc2 Pridiyathorn said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is prepared to handle the effects of the change in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, and it shouldn't have any impact on the Thai financial system.  \par \par \uc2 Thai markets and financial institutions are closed Friday for a public holiday.  \par \par \uc2 Beijing has initially revalued the yuan 2.1% to CNY8.11 to the dollar, which probably isn't enough to satisfy those in the U.S. who believe that the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's undervaluation gives the country's products an unfair advantage in global markets.  \par \par \uc2 'The important point isn't about how much the yuan has changed,' Pridiyathorn said. 'It's that China is willing to adjust and allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tie up with other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'  \par \par \uc2 Usara Wilaipich, an economist in Bangkok for Standard Chartered Bank, said the initial reaction to the yuan revaluation will come in the form of short-term capital inflows to the region, which have already boosted the baht and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Beyond that, Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other regional central banks may not need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as much to protect the competitiveness of their countries' exports.  \par \par \uc2 'I think it will help relax \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pressure because the yuan revaluation will help reduce pressure. Most Asian countries previously were concerned about competitiveness in terms of trade,' she said.  \par \par \uc2 Although it's unclear how much less undervalued the yuan will be, since \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and their relative representation in the yuan basket are unknown, a stronger yuan should also improve the export competitiveness of Thailand and other Asian countries, she said.  \par \par \uc2 Further ahead, however, the benefits for Thailand and other regional suppliers of China's massive export industry are less clear.  \par \par \uc2 If the Chinese economy or its export growth slows as a result of the yuan's appreciation, the fall in income for its suppliers could be a greater factor for them than the price advantage provided by more competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.  \par \par \uc2 'Exports from (Thailand and) the region to China could slow down, and in that case, the income effect is larger than the price effect,' Usara said. -By Tom Fox and Suttinee Yuvejwattana, Dow Jones Newswires; 662 266 0744; djnews.bangkok@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (Grant Peck of the Associated Press contributed to this article.)  \par \par \uc2 -0-  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020050721e17l00anf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
