\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Won, Taiwan dlr to outshine other Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dhara Ranasinghe   \par \uc2 859 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2005\par 08:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, June 17 (Reuters) - It was not that long ago when all Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  looked good in the eyes of investors. Exports were booming across the region and speculation was high that China would allow the yuan to rise.   \par \par \uc2 But now, with the U.S. dollar on a roll, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts say investors need to be more picky. The South Korean won and Taiwan dollar still have a lot going for them, but southeast Asian units such as the Thai baht are expected to underperform.   \par \par \uc2 "Generally, northeast Asia is more tapped into the Chinese economy, which is still showing quite a good performance," said Philip Wee, senior market strategist at DBS in Singapore.   \par \par \uc2 "Use of the northeast \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as proxies for the yuan has been quite evident."   \par \par \uc2 Half way into the year and the dollar, helped by rising U.S interest rates, has gained over six percent against the yen and almost double that against the euro. Its gains have allowed the dollar to escape a downtrend that was in its third year.   \par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have taken a bruising as a result. The won <KRW=KFTC> hit an eight-week low and the Singapore dollar <SGD=> dropped to an eight-month trough this week.   \par \par \uc2 But analysts say regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially those from northeast Asia, should hold their ground against the U.S. dollar from here, particularly as speculation continues that China will reform its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime in the months ahead.   \par \par \uc2 The yuan <CNY=CFXS> is pegged in a tight band near 8.28 per dollar and China's trading partners have urged the country to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate, arguing the yuan is undervalued and gives Chinese exporters an unfair advantage.   \par \par \uc2 If China allows the yuan to rise, other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are expected to rise too and that means investors are reluctant to sell regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  too much.   \par \par \uc2 GOING WITH THE FLOW   \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the won and Taiwan dollar would benefit most from China speculation because of their countries' increasingly strong trade links to China.   \par \par \uc2 Trade and investment between Taiwan and China has boomed since the late 1980s. Exports to China accounted for about 20 percent of total South Korean exports in 2004, from under five percent in 1990, JP Morgan noted in a recent report.   \par \par \uc2 Speculation about what China will do with its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also boosted inflows of foreign capital into the region.   \par \par \uc2 India and Taiwan have seen the heaviest net foreign buying of equities in the region in the past 250 days, data released this week by Nomura International shows.   \par \par \uc2 The strength of demand for Taiwan shares has taken many analysts by surprise.   \par \par \uc2 John Cairns, head of research for Asia at IDEAglobal in Singapore, said portfolio equity inflows had been expected to slow after a recent weighting upgrade by Morgan Stanley Capital International, whose indices heavily influence share buying.   \par \par \uc2 "But the inflows have actually continued, so that is keeping the Taiwan dollar quite resilient," Cairns said.   \par \par \uc2 TRADE BARRIER   \par \par \uc2 The key factor determining which \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will resist dollar strength and which ones will not, says JP Morgan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Claudio Piron, is each country's current account.   \par \par \uc2 Since 1998, Asia has had a large trade surplus but some countries are starting to see their surpluses either narrow or move into deficit as oil prices remain persistently high, pushing up the price of imports, while exports to China slow.   \par \par \uc2 "Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are a bit more vulnerable at this point as they are backed by narrowing trade surpluses and in some case deficits," said UBS \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Bhanu Baweja.   \par \par \uc2 India's trade deficit widened to $7.21 billion in the first two months of the fiscal year to March 31, 2006. Thailand's current account recorded a deficit in March and April after a small surplus in February.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts said Thailand was on course for an annual current account deficit in 2005 and that would be the first since the Asian financial crisis.   \par \par \uc2 "Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should continue to perform quite well but the baht is our underperformer," said IDEAglobal's Cairns.   \par \par \uc2 "This is mostly because of the surplus moving into a deficit. Bank of Thailand policy is also important," he said. "They don't seem too willing to come in at the top end and support the baht."   \par \par \uc2 Asian central banks have a reputation for being heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 interveners\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, but \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief said this week the baht was not too weak against the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The baht <THB=>, Asia's worst performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year after the yen, hit a seven-month low versus the dollar on Friday.   \par \par \uc2 Significant outperformance of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar was also seen as unlikely as rising U.S. interest rates suggest investors may get better returns by holding dollars.   \par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve's key interest rate is at 3.0 percent and expected to rise further. That compares to Singapore, where rates are just above 2.0 percent, and Taiwan, where they are just below there.   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|SI|Z|J|PHD|IF|SNI|SNS   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050617e16h000fi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
